1.Create a table comparing Agile and Waterfall models across 5 factors relevant to software testing, such as requirements, feedback cycle, tester involvement, documentation, and flexibility.
Answer :
1>Requirements
Agile: Requirements can change during development based on customer needs.
Waterfall: Requirements are mostly fixed at the beginning of the project.
2>Feedback Cycle
Agile: Feedback is received continuously after each sprint.
Waterfall: Feedback is generally received after a major phase or at the end of development.
3>Tester Involvement
Agile: Testers are involved from the beginning and test features during every sprint.
Waterfall: Testers usually become involved after the development phase is completed.
4>Documentation
Agile: Focuses on necessary documentation while giving more importance to working software.
Waterfall: Requires detailed documentation at every development phase.
5>Flexibility
Agile: Highly flexible; changes can be added to upcoming sprints.
Waterfall: Less flexible; changes after development has started can be difficult and costly.


2.Pick any app you use daily (like Instagram, Zomato, or Paytm) and describe how the testing process would look different if the team used Agile instead of Waterfall. Mention at least 3 specific testing activities or practices that would change.
Answer :
Testing Instagram Using Agile Instead of Waterfall

If Instagram used Agile, testing would happen continuously during every sprint instead of waiting until the entire application is developed.

>>Three major changes would be:
1)Continuous Testing: Testers would test features such as login, stories, and messaging during the same sprint in which they are developed.
2)Early Test Case Design: Testers would create test cases as soon as user stories are discussed, rather than waiting for the complete application.
3)Continuous Regression Testing: Automated regression tests would be run frequently to make sure new features do not break existing features.



